# rm267, the stall measured in elbows (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm267
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_soren_coaldale
written_by codex
title the stall measured in elbows
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=carter
traits temper=short talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk measures_a_dream_stall_with_an_elbow_in_any_empty_space
likes mutton_stew digging market_bustle summer_evenings
dislikes haggling rats
fears a_slipping_strap a_partner_who_moves_the_marks
hopes a_stall_under_cover an_hour_with_the_spade
topics work +0.4 prices +0.4 pastimes +0.3 home +0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=blunt refuse=firm tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# measures empty corners with an elbow, saves towards a covered stall, and would set a load in the muck before accepting a change of price halfway across the square.

words
oath! = Sore pad and slant board | Splinters in my pitch
saying! = Agree the lift before the lifting | A stall starts with elbow room
pitch = my imaginary pitch | my chalked-out stall
measure = the span of my forearm | one bare elbow's length
loosecord = a strap with slack left | a hanging strap end
graftwood = a dry forked stick | a notch of hazel
carryingpad = the folded shoulder cloth | my old carrying pad
footbreadth = a shoe's width | a paw of room
coinwrap = my little cloth purse | my stall money
shovelcut = a spade's breadth | a clean wedge of earth
lastleaf = a bruised cabbage leaf | one broken outer leaf
cornermark = a short chalk hook | a shallow heel mark
stewpiece = a brown piece of mutton | a scrap of mutton in broth
railend = the end of the front board | the square rail end
duckboard = a split walking board | a scrap of raised flooring
handroom = room to bend my elbows | space enough for a turn
rowcover = an old sack spread flat | a rag of sacking

any
opinion @1 church for 1 : I've lifted flour sacks enough to remember the weight. The chapel has put plenty of that weight into hungry bellies.
opinion @1 city for 1 : There's only one square where my elbow knows all four corners. I'd miss it.
opinion @1 dole for 2 : I used to think everybody in that queue had time to spare. Try missing a shift for a loaf and see what it costs.
opinion @1 road for 1 : The road puts weight in my barrow and a bit of brass in my pocket. I won't call it cruel on a fair day.
opinion @1 houses against 2 : The House mark faces out on the bale. The scrape down my shoulder faces in, where no fine wolf sees it.
opinion @1 watch against 1 : A cart blocking a Sump doorway will stand there all afternoon. Strange what the Watch finds time to move.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : A price offered after the load is lifted is never the one I'd have agreed while it stood on the ground.
opinion @1 festivals against 1 : Let the music come after the market. You can't hear a warning over a horn.

grown
line @1 greet : Come stand beside the marks. There's clear space.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : Keep your toes off the chalk and we're well met.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : First light makes this square look twice its size. Welcome.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Enough said by a nod at this hour.
line @5 greet [self.working=yes] : Welcome along my empty side.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There's room for a familiar pair of elbows alongside mine.
line @1 again.open : We've gone all the way round to one another.
line @1 greet.return : Your voice came clear over here.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : I've an elbow free for a familiar nudge.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : Much obliged. I'll answer properly when the load's down.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Can hear you better than see you, but welcome.
line @1 farewell : Keep the weight close on the way.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll keep my chalk. You keep your footing.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Pick the lighter patch at the bend.
line @1 react.news : I'm listening. Tell it at the pace it happened.
line @1 react.news.new : That's another mark to put down.
line @1 react.news.known : I'd counted that bit in already.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Whose shoulder was under it when it happened?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : I'd spare the weight of a small errand for that house.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : A nice broad piece of news to set out in the sun.
line @1 answer.how : Worn about the shoulders. Sound underfoot.
line @2 answer.how [self.working=yes] : I've room to move a load and small reason to complain.
line @1 ask.how : Any part of today still riding heavy on you?
line @1 sympathise : A hard day doesn't finish when the weight comes off.
line @1 reassure : Put one corner down here. You'll breathe easier.
line @1 decline : No extra carrying till we agree the whole distance.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair. I'll shift my mark back.
line @1 thank : My shoulder knows exactly what that favour's worth.
line @1 deflect_thanks : You can give somebody else the easy end next time.
line @1 preclose : I'm beginning to measure the space beyond you.
line @2 preclose [self.working=yes] : Can't leave this weight hanging on a corner.
line @1 accept_preclose : Carry on. My elbows are well clear.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : A familiar shoulder! This way a moment.
line @1 ack : Square enough.
scene elbow
when topic=pastimes|dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I measured a stall against a blank wall this morning. | There's a whole counter in the space my elbow covers three times.
b : Have you space for a chair? | How far does that reach?
a : My first plan left me outside in the wet. I've allowed another elbow. | Just far enough to slide the price across without leaning on the goods.

scene price
when topic=work self.job=carter self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The price carries the load exactly as far as we agreed. | I make a mark where the carrying ends.
b : Even for one extra step? | How can you mark somebody else's floor?
a : An extra step I might give. An extra flight I expect to be asked about. | With my eye. Last blue shutter, first dry sill. Something we both recognise.

scene braise
when topic=food self.likes=mutton_stew
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Take the lid off mutton too often and a pot loses its temper. | I want my stew thick enough to stay put while I blow on it.
b : Have you heard a pot complain? | What's wrong with thin broth?
a : That little hiss when you put the lid back. Supper's asked you to leave it alone. | A draught carries it off the spoon. My mouth ought to get it before the wind does.

scene ditch
when topic=pastimes self.likes=digging
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep the first spadeful of good earth to fill the hole back in. | I spread rough sacking beside the patch I'm digging.
b : Why save that one? | For kneeling?
a : That's the good earth. The rough stuff underneath can go to the bottom. | For the good topsoil. My knees can fend for themselves.

scene crowd
when topic=smalltalk self.likes=market_bustle
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Nobody in the square looks the same height with a bale against each shoulder. | Market noise sounds different down near barrow height.
b : Can you see who's coming? | How different?
a : I see feet. A foot turned sideways often says a body's stopped. | More wheels than voices. Trouble squeaks before it shouts.

scene latebench
when topic=day here.time=dusk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep the evening for looking at the square when the corners are clear. | At dusk a stall casts a better-sized shadow than a House sign.
b : What do you see with the stalls cleared? | How can a shadow be a better size?
a : Where I could put my own. Half the corners are bigger in my head than on the ground. | Wide enough for a bowl and a pair of tired feet. That's my measure.

scene rats
when topic=animals self.dislikes=rats
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I stand grain on blocks with bare air beneath. | I sweep a half-circle round a stacked sack.
b : Can a rat climb a block? | Why only half a circle?
a : A rat could climb my hat. I'm after seeing loose grains before they bring a crowd. | The wall takes care of the other half. Anything disturbed shows plain next day.

scene stubborn
when topic=prices self.dislikes=haggling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : When somebody offers less twice I put both straps down. | I don't ask a customer to bid against an empty square.
b : Then what? | Would you earn a bit more that way?
a : Then there's a quiet space where an honest answer ought to go. | By making the customer worry, perhaps. It's heavy enough earning it straight.

scene gardenweather
when topic=weather here.season=summer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A long light evening gives a spade a second shift. | I like summer light on turned earth.
b : Do your arms agree? | Is that when you plant?
a : One row usually agrees. The second has to wait till my shoulders feel more agreeable. | Sometimes I just look. Not every good hour has a price nailed to it.

scene stalled
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My stall has had three roofs this week, all in my mind. | Sometimes I dream somebody has moved my counter while I slept.
b : Which roof will you keep? | How far can a dream move it?
a : The one that sheds water out the back. I'm trying to keep the customers. | Only a foot. Precisely enough that none of my elbows measures up.

scene sill
when topic=home self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Every Sump room I measure has one awkward bit behind the door. | A carrying pad left high dries on both faces.
b : What fits an awkward corner? | Why bother with the underside?
a : Put the spade there, handle up. No use throwing away room a spade will occupy. | Because that's the face my skin will meet tomorrow.

scene watchstrap
when topic=work self.job=carter
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I turn a damp strap over and look at the pale crease. | A barrow handle ought to have two clear places for paws.
b : Does a crease mean it's worn out? | Surely the whole handle is for holding.
a : If it cracks when I flatten it, somebody can have it for tying light bundles. | Hang a sack off the middle of one and tell me how much grip's left on a bend.

scene rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Rain puts a cheap shine on a poor floorboard. | That sound of water falling off a roof means I need to look up before I shift a sack.
b : Does the shine hide much? | Why up?
a : Splits, grease and one or two nail heads. That's enough. | The bottom of the sack gets under shelter. Its top might be directly under the drip.

scene friend
when topic=smalltalk self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd give #self.friend# first sight of the stall when I get it. | I've marked out a place for a second cup.
b : First pick of the stock too? | Who gets the cup?
a : First sight means first sight. Goods are another matter entirely. | I'd offer it to #self.friend#. Somebody has to tell me whether I've made the counter too high.

scene marketage
when topic=memories self.age>=35
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My elbow hasn't changed length since I started counting with it. | In my younger days I thought the taller wolf got the long end of a load.
b : Has the market changed its size? | And now?
a : My plans have. As a younger wolf I wanted four elbows just for the sign. | Now I look at which end weighs most before I admire anybody's height.

scene housemark
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : If a bale's stamped, I still turn it and inspect the seam. | A good seal is no help under a bad strap.
b : You don't trust a mark? | Who gets the blame if the strap goes?
a : A mark may tell me who owns the goods. It doesn't keep my toes from underneath. | Ask once the bale is standing on the ground. While it's slipping I want another shoulder.

scene passing
when topic=travel self.job=carter
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I take a light first trip along an unfamiliar stretch. | If a stair turns back on itself I go up empty first.
b : Doesn't that waste a journey? | What's the empty trip meant to show you?
a : It saves the trip I might otherwise make headfirst. | The place where the barrow has to stop being a barrow and the sacks go on a back.

scene store
when topic=prices self.wealth=poor|destitute
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep my stall money separate from the ordinary coppers. | A copper saved for a pitch is as heavy as any other.
b : In a separate purse? | Does it feel different?
a : In #coinwrap#. A tight knot makes me stop and consider before raiding it for supper. | In the pocket, no. In the mind it's got half a plank hanging off it.

scene markout
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I draw two widths for a doorway when I plan my counter. | I've allowed my dream stall a row of splinters already.
b : Why two? | Before you've even bought the boards?
a : One to enter with an empty back and one to come out carrying. I buy boards for the wider of the two. | Yes. Good to reckon on losing a little time to sandpaper.

scene chill
when topic=health
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The corner of my shoulder pad is thicker than the middle. | The best stuffing for a carrying pad is the bit that stays put.
b : Which part wears harder? | Does stuffing move?
a : Neither at first. Once the corner starts rubbing I turn it in, and that's how the thickness comes. | Everything moves under a sack. Useful things don't move very far.

scene lanes
when topic=land self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A narrow Sump lane is wide enough until two wolves try to be first. | I pick the wide landing for any conversation that needs my whole face.
b : Who ought to wait? | Can't you talk sideways?
a : Whichever can stand still without losing balance. This isn't a question of rank. | Yes, but the load doesn't know when my head is turning.

scene sprouts
when topic=season self.likes=digging
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I stick a forked twig where I've sown the smallest seeds. | New earth has an excellent way of looking empty.
b : Does the fork point somewhere? | Have you dug up your own sowing?
a : At the ground I'm supposed to leave alone, however bare it looks. | Just far enough to see a pale root. I covered the root gently and dug elsewhere.

scene music
when topic=festival
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd put the loudest player on an empty handcart. | A festival bench should have a clear lane at either end.
b : So the music can travel? | How much room do you want?
a : So anybody moving goods sees the noise coming and gets a quiet minute once it's gone. | Enough to get a hot bowl through without soaking a coat in mutton.

scene skies
when topic=day here.time=dawn
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A cart standing full in the early light throws a crooked shadow if it's leaning. | I check yesterday's heel marks before the first heavy tread wipes them out.
b : Is the shadow enough warning? | What good are old marks?
a : Enough to make me lay a paw on each wheel before I take the handles. | They tell me where the boards dipped. A fresh mark can lie about yesterday.

scene bargain
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A wolf who doesn't know the square may mistake a shout for an offer. | I draw a plain line for a newcomer before I agree to lift anything.
b : How do you make yourself understood? | Across what?
a : Point to where we stand, point to where we finish. Then say the price slowly. | Across the ground where the carrying ends. One straight scrape works in plenty of accents.

scene faith
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like hearing bowls scrape outside a chapel door on a dole day. | At the chapel supper I stack the bowls first and give thanks after.
b : Doesn't the noise spoil the quiet? | Why after the stacking?
a : No. A noisy queue means folk are being fed. A silent one worries me more. | It frees the bench for the next hungry wolf. That's a thanks with a little weight to it.

scene storm
when topic=dreams|weather
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I picture the worst gust when I plan my stall cover. | My imaginary counter has never lost a single board in a gale.
b : How high would you pitch it? | Is it built that well?
a : Low enough to nip the wind. I'll duck myself before I ask a roof to fly. | Hasn't been built at all. That's been wonderful for the cost of repairs.

scene apprent
when topic=work self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The first trick worth learning is to admit a sack is too heavy. | I'd teach a beginner how to set a weight down before trying to lift it.
b : How would you tell before lifting? | Wouldn't that lesson run backwards?
a : Lean it first and feel whether it means to follow you. You can stop there. | I'd start with something light already on a bench. No sense giving a lesson that ends on a toe.

story one_elbow [topic=memories]
  : I once chalked out my whole stall in a patch of sunlight.
  : By the time I'd marked the last rail the sun had moved and half the stall was in shadow.
  b : Which half would you keep?
  : Both. The shadow side did nicely for the mutton.

story button [topic=food self.likes=mutton_stew]
  : A bit of carrot in my stew looked exactly like a brass button.
  : I kept pushing it round the bowl, thinking I'd find the fastening holes.
  b : Did you find any?
  : I bit it at last. Two dents from my teeth looked close enough.

story wrongwidth [topic=work self.job=carter]
  : I measured a narrow opening with both arms spread and pronounced it wide enough.
  : Then I went to push a barrow through and remembered I hadn't measured the barrow.
  b : Did the load have to go back?
  : No. It came through parcel by parcel. Gave me time to remember several other things I ought to have measured.

story splitseed [topic=pastimes self.likes=digging]
  : I planted two little rows from the same twist of seed and covered one with sacking.
  : The covered row came first through the earth. I lifted the sack to admire the seedlings properly.
  b : Did they keep their lead?
  : Till a bird came to admire them in its own manner. I left the other row to itself.

story buyspace [topic=dreams]
  : I set a coin down where a stall board might go, then another at the far corner.
  : Up came a gust that blew my plan away. The coppers stayed put.
  b : The stall was only on paper then?
  : Yes. It's reassuring to know the money weighs more than the dream. I gather both and start over.

rule @loads
when concept=bark self.job=carter self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Make room for the long end first. | A shoulder's paid for one trip at a time.

rule @garden
when concept=bark self.likes=digging
does aside
cooldown 8h
say The fork marks seed. Mind your heel. | Don't bury the little twig.

rule @whenrain
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Sack corner's in the drip, lift it clear. | Dry board, slippery edge.

rule @market
when concept=bark here.place=market
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Settle the distance while the goods are down. | Clear one elbow more, please.

rule @dark
when concept=bark here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Feel for the high stone before the turn. | One lamp can't cover two cart lengths.

rule @rat
when concept=bark self.dislikes=rats
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Leave air between the sack and the wall. | Sweep that spill before the whiskers find it.

dlg @1 resident.hub : There's a strip clear beside me. Speak from there.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : I've measured your elbow before, #topic.player#. Welcome.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : I haven't agreed to carry a thing. You may speak.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : The ground's only just visible here. Stay in the lighter patch.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=carter] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I name a price with both ends of the journey in mind.
dlg @2 resident.job : I've a shoulder that knows when to take the strain.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Here's what has made it as far as my patch. #world.news#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : I can hear the stir about #world.event_words# from the square.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : There'll be gaps in the stacks after that cart was stripped.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : A wedding! Gives a new pair of shoulders reason to share the weight.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.death=recent] : I'd leave a way clear for the house with a death to bear.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : Nobody's brought me anything I'd repeat even for a bright new barrow.
dlg @1 resident.bye : There's your space in the stream. Go while it's clear.
graft @elbow_pitch resident.hub
choice How big is the stall you mean to have? -> elbow_pitch
node elbow_pitch
say Stretch your arm. Now fold it and measure with the elbow. That much, three times, and another bit for a mug that needn't be moved every time I turn.
choice Will a mug really get a place of its own? -> elbow_pitch_more
choice Leave the chalk mark alone.  -> hub
node elbow_pitch_more
say Yes. I've spent proper coin on tea and drunk it cold for lack of a safe place to set it. One of my own boards ought to keep a mug safe.
choice Leave the chalk mark alone.  -> hub
choice Step away from the wall. -> bye

graft @which_weight resident.hub
choice Which end of a load should I take? -> which_weight
node which_weight
say Show me how it stands on the ground. The end that wants to stay there is generally heavier. You won't always guess it by looking.
choice Would you let me take the lighter end? -> which_weight_more
choice Set the weight down gently. -> hub
node which_weight_more
say Gladly. I'd sooner you carried your share ten good paces than shifted mine three and dropped the lot.
choice Set the weight down gently. -> hub
choice Head into the square. -> bye
